WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:03.730
A linear operator preserves addition and scalar multiplication

00:00:03.730 --> 00:00:10.390
On the plane, stretching the first coordinate by two while leaving the second unchanged is linear

00:00:10.390 --> 00:00:17.467
The operator norm measures the largest possible output size for an input of norm at most one.

00:00:18.767 --> 00:00:24.838
For Euclidean length, this transformation enlarges no vector by more than a factor of two

00:00:24.838 --> 00:00:30.910
The first coordinate unit vector achieves exactly that factor, so the operator norm is two

00:00:30.910 --> 00:00:35.767
A single successful vector supplies the lower bound, not the upper bound.

00:00:37.067 --> 00:00:42.360
A continuous linear functional outputs a scalar and belongs to the dual space

00:00:42.360 --> 00:00:46.024
This functional is the dot product with one, two

00:00:46.024 --> 00:00:54.167
Cauchy Schwarz bounds its magnitude by square root five times the input norm, and a parallel unit vector attains equality.

00:00:55.467 --> 00:00:58.195
Linearity alone does not imply boundedness

00:00:58.195 --> 00:01:10.474
On continuously differentiable functions over zero to two pi, using the uniform norm for inputs and outputs, differentiation sends these shrinking functions to derivatives of size one

00:01:10.474 --> 00:01:14.567
No fixed amplification bound can work for every n.
